  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Minimal pre-Thanksgiving travel weather impacts \n\nA colder NW flow behind the morning cold front reinforced by a \nfast moving shortwave trough crossing the Great Lakes will \npromote the development of scattered rain and snow showers \ntonight downwind of Lake Erie. At this point, it looks like most\nshowers will stay north of I-80. Lowering inversion heights in \nresponse to eastward migrating 1030+mb high pressure will bring \na quick end to the lake effect precip early in the day on \nTuesday, leading to a 24-36 hour period of quiet/dry weather and\ngood holiday travel conditions. Tuesday night may be the \ncoldest night of the season so far as mainly clear, Beaver \nSupermoon-lit skies and light winds will allow min temps to fall\ninto the 15 to 25F range. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Wet and windy Thanksgiving Day\n\nWidespread soaking 1+ inch rainfall expected on Thanksgiving \nDay as a very strong easterly low level flow associated with a \npowerful coastal storm system transports anomalous moisture \ninto CPA. Model consensus timing suggests precip crosses the MD \nline around or shortly after 12Z/7AM Thursday, but there is\nstill some uncertainty regarding start time. Depending on how \nfast the precipitation arrives, there may still be enough \nretreating cold air in place to support a mix of rain and snow \non the front-end before changing to all rain by the afternoon,\nbut this would be limited to the higher elevations of the\nnorthern tier, and even there, wintry impacts are not expected.\nThe heaviest rain is projected to fall between 7AM Thu and 1AM \nFri. \n\nThe potential for strong wind gusts remains a concern with this\nsystem, particularly over the southeastern third of the CWA. \nRecent runs of some of the deterministic models, including the \nGFS and ECMWF, have shown a 60 to 80 knot 850 mb low-level \neasterly jet developing in response to the strong pressure \ngradient between the deepening low to the south and retreating \nhigh pressure to the north. It remains to be seen how much of \nthis wind will be able to make it down to the surface, but \nsoundings from the GFS show 45-50 knot winds at the top of the \nmixed layer Thursday afternoon over the Lower Susquehanna \nValley. The NBM 24 hour maximum wind gust product also shows \nbetter than 50/50 odds of wind gusts exceeding 45 mph or \nadvisory criteria over Lancaster County. Of note, the strong \nwinds on Thursday will be from the east which anecdotally does \ntend to cause more tree damage since trees are \"tested\" a lot \nless frequently from the east than from the north or west in \nthis region. Unfortunately, isolated to scattered power outages\nwill be possible on Thanksgiving, especially in the Lower Susq \nValley where winds are strongest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Breezy on Friday with rain showers/elevation snow \n\nColder air will gradually wrap back into Pennsylvania late \nThursday night and Friday as the system stacks up along the\nNorth-Central Appalachians. This should allow for snow to begin\nmixing in especially over the higher terrain from the Laurel\nHighlands northward along the Allegheny Plateau into northern\nPA. Rain/snow mix during the day Friday beneath the stacked low\ntransitions to more of a lake effect/upslope precip pattern \nFriday night into Saturday as the system lifts into New England.\nIt will remain breezy into the holiday weekend with west winds \ngusting 25-35 mph.",
